# TTP-2026-0166 — Kerberoast Weak-Ticket Burst Followed by Pass-the-Hash — Cortex AD Behavioral IOC (ABIOC) (T1558.003 / T1550.002)
# XQL paste-buffer generated by detection_scanner/scripts/export_artifacts.py

## ABIOC — A user received multiple weakly encrypted service tickets (behavioral-ML, causality-anchored)
# severity: high
# behavioral_profile: machine-learning
# causality_anchor: Learned per-identity service-ticket-encryption baseline (AES-only, human cadence) -> a burst of RC4-HMAC (0x17) service tickets across many SPNs for the same account, anchored to the requesting identity as the root of the roast-to-Pass-the-Hash chain.
# A single account receives an unusual burst of Kerberos service tickets (4769) encrypted with legacy RC4-HMAC (TicketEncryptionType 0x17) across many distinct Service Principal Names, deviating from the AES-only service-ticket-encryption baseline Cortex learned for the identity. Every ticket request is a valid, well-formed Kerberos operation and no single 4769 is a known-bad IOC; the auto-tuned ABIOC fires on the weak-encryption-ticket-burst deviation from the account's learned baseline — the offline-crackable roast material — anchored to the requesting-identity causality chain rather than to any static indicator.

dataset = msft_windows_security
| filter event_id = 4769
| filter ticket_encryption_type = "0x17"
| alter principal_name = arrayindex(split(target_user_name, "@"), 0)
| comp count() as weak_ticket_count, count_distinct(service_name) as distinct_services, values(service_name) as services, min(_time) as first_seen, max(_time) as last_seen by principal_name
| alter window_minutes = divide(subtract(last_seen, first_seen), 60000000)
| filter weak_ticket_count >= 5 and distinct_services >= 3
| fields principal_name, weak_ticket_count, distinct_services, services, window_minutes, first_seen, last_seen

## ABIOC — A user requested multiple service tickets (behavioral-ML, causality-anchored)
# severity: medium
# behavioral_profile: statistical
# causality_anchor: Learned per-identity service-ticket request rate (few SPNs, human cadence) -> a high-volume 4769 request burst across many distinct SPNs by the same account, anchored to the requesting identity that drove the weak-ticket roast.
# The same account requests Kerberos service tickets (4769) across an anomalously large set of distinct Service Principal Names in a short window — the enumeration-and-request volume of a Kerberoasting sweep — exceeding the low, human-cadence service-ticket request rate Cortex learned for the identity. The alert fires on ticket-request VOLUME (independent of encryption strength) via a statistical deviation from the learned baseline, anchored to the same requesting identity as the weak-ticket burst.

dataset = msft_windows_security
| filter event_id = 4769
| alter principal_name = arrayindex(split(target_user_name, "@"), 0)
| comp count() as ticket_requests, count_distinct(service_name) as distinct_services, min(_time) as first_seen, max(_time) as last_seen by principal_name
| alter window_minutes = divide(subtract(last_seen, first_seen), 60000000)
| filter distinct_services >= 5 and ticket_requests >= 8 and window_minutes <= 30
| fields principal_name, ticket_requests, distinct_services, window_minutes, first_seen, last_seen

## ABIOC — A user authenticated with weak NTLM to multiple hosts (behavioral-ML, causality-anchored)
# severity: high
# behavioral_profile: machine-learning
# causality_anchor: Weak-ticket roast burst on the identity -> an NTLM (NtLmSsp) network logon by the same account that deviates from its learned Kerberos-interactive authentication baseline, anchored to the roasted identity as the lateral-movement stage of one credential-abuse chain.
# An account authenticates with legacy NTLM over an NTLM (type 3) network logon through the NtLmSsp logon process — presenting a password-derived secret directly, with no preceding Kerberos exchange — the Pass-the-Hash (T1550.002) lateral-movement shape — against an account whose learned authentication baseline is Kerberos interactive logon. Each NTLM logon is technically valid and benign in isolation; the auto-tuned ABIOC fires on the out-of-baseline weak-NTLM network authentication deviation, aggregating the distinct target hosts reached, anchored to the roasted identity whose service-ticket material was harvested moments earlier.

dataset = msft_windows_security
| filter event_id = 4624
| filter logon_type = 3
| filter authentication_package_name = "NTLM"
| filter logon_process_name contains "NtLmSsp"
| alter principal_name = arrayindex(split(target_user_name, "@"), 0)
| comp count() as ntlm_network_logons, count_distinct(host_name) as target_hosts, values(host_name) as hosts, min(_time) as first_seen, max(_time) as last_seen by principal_name
| filter ntlm_network_logons >= 1
| fields principal_name, ntlm_network_logons, target_hosts, hosts, first_seen, last_seen

## VALIDATION — SIM-ITDR-020 Windows Security Kerberos and NTLM Logon Events Landed
# purpose: validation
# dataset: msft_windows_security

dataset = msft_windows_security
| filter _time > to_timestamp(current_time() - 1800)
| filter event_id in (4769, 4624)
| comp count() as security_events, count_distinct(event_id) as distinct_event_ids, values(event_id) as event_ids by host_name
| filter security_events >= 1
| fields host_name, security_events, distinct_event_ids, event_ids

